# Contributing

## Types but not TypeScript

Types have to be defined and are enforced but instead of writing TypeScript files we use plain JavaScript with
JSDoc to use types. This allows us to write safe code without needing an additional compilation step.
Tooling like VS Code picks automatically picks up `jsconfig.json` which is equivalent to `tsconfig.json` and helps you
write type safe code. Meaning the code would still pass `tsc` if it were run.

## Package manager

This project uses pnpm. The exact version is pinned in the `packageManager` field of `package.json`, and CI
reads it from there, so there is one place to bump it. Install dependencies with `pnpm install`.

## Versioning

Versions are controlled using git tags which get picked up by GitHub actions. No need to manually update
`package.json` — it stays at `0.0.0` and the release workflow sets the real version from the tag before packing.

Tags carry no `v` prefix: `0.6.1`, not `v0.6.1`.

## Releasing

Releasing is a two-part flow: pushing a tag builds and *stages* the release, and a maintainer then approves it
with their second factor. CI can never publish on its own — see [Why it works this way](#why-it-works-this-way).

### One-time setup

- **A signing key.** The tag ruleset requires signed tags, so an unsigned `git tag` is rejected on push.
  Either GPG or SSH signing works; for SSH:

  ```sh
  git config --global gpg.format ssh
  git config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
  ```

  Add the same key to <https://github.com/settings/keys> as a **signing key** (a key registered only for
  authentication will not verify tags).

- **npm 11.15 or newer**, if you want to approve releases from the CLI rather than the website:

  ```sh
  npm --version
  ```

  Older versions have no `npm stage` command. The website works regardless of local npm version.

### 1. Tag the release

Tag creation is restricted to repository admins. Update `CHANGELOG`/`README` if the release needs it, then:

```sh
git tag -s 0.6.1 -m 'Release 0.6.1'
```

```sh
git push origin 0.6.1
```

### 2. Let CI build and stage it

Pushing the tag runs [`.github/workflows/publish.yaml`](.github/workflows/publish.yaml), which

1. runs the tests,
2. builds the type definitions into `dist/` in a **separate job** and passes them along as an artifact,
3. in a third job that installs no dependencies at all, sets the version from the tag and runs
   `npm stage publish`.

Watch it at <https://github.com/SantaClaas/material-tailwind/actions>. Staging is not publishing: nothing is
installable from the registry yet.

### 3. Approve the staged release

This is the manual step, and it needs your 2FA — it is the reason a compromised CI cannot ship a release.

On the website: npmjs.com → user menu → **Staged Packages** → review → approve.

Or from the CLI:

```sh
npm stage list
```

```sh
npm stage view @claas.dev/material-tailwind@0.6.1
```

```sh
npm stage approve @claas.dev/material-tailwind@0.6.1
```

To inspect the exact tarball before approving, `npm stage download` fetches it. To throw the release away,
`npm stage reject` — then fix the problem and tag a new patch version. Do not reuse a tag: releases are
immutable and tag updates are blocked by the ruleset.

### 4. Verify

```sh
npm view @claas.dev/material-tailwind version
```

The release should carry a provenance attestation linking it back to the workflow run that built it.

## Why it works this way

There is no npm token anywhere — not in CI, not in repo secrets. Instead:

- The package is set up on npm with **Trusted Publishing**, so only this repository's `publish.yaml` can
  stage a release, authenticated per-run via OIDC. Publishing access is set to require 2FA and disallow
  tokens, so there is no token left to steal.
- Trusted Publishing is restricted to **stage-only**. Even a fully compromised workflow can stage something,
  but a human with a second factor has to approve it before anyone can install it.
- The publish job installs **no dependencies** and uses no cache — anything running in a job that holds
  `id-token: write` could otherwise publish. Building happens in a different job with no such permission.
- All third-party actions are pinned to a commit SHA, and installs run with `--ignore-scripts`.
- New dependency versions are held for three days (`minimumReleaseAge` in `pnpm-workspace.yaml`) so that
  malicious releases have time to be taken down before they reach a build.
- Tag creation, update and deletion are restricted to repository admins, and releases are immutable.

[`.github/workflows/check-workflows.yaml`](.github/workflows/check-workflows.yaml) runs
[zizmor](https://docs.zizmor.sh) over the workflows on every push and pull request to keep these properties
from eroding.
